2186074659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7809af08-83ac-5450-986d-2614a3fd22d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1061 197419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "413b9b90-7693-5568-a868-970271a5a258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1061 197420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99e9f36f-648c-5025-bfca-c63f6f77d9b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074659 #edb/ref 1061 197424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30b3f613-45c5-50c5-bd79-5c12149aea27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=4 \\\\ x-2=y\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+(x-2) & =4 \\\\ 2x-2 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\ y & =3-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as $\\left(3, 1\\right).$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1065 8131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1e8cb2d-3d4e-5cb9-8947-2bd9deb11b89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1039 "Cross-Multiplying with One Binomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3ff2f965-b406-5c9a-bd41-73ccdce951eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 1759